    "innovation": "This paper presents the first observation-based, Greenland-wide estimate of ice mass loss since AD 1900 by using historical aerial imagery to map the ice sheet's maximum extent during the Little Ice Age, quantifying its substantial contribution to twentieth-century sea-level rise.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "laser altimetry",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "ICESat",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "2003\u20132010 elevation changes from air- and space-borne laser altimetry. To detect ice surface elevation changes from April 2003 to April 2010, which serves as the base data set from which to calculate a scale value, we use all available Ice, Cloud, and land Elevation Satellite (ICESat) GLA12 Release 31 data36. ICESat elevations have a crossover standard deviation of ICESat = 0.2 m (refs 37-39).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "laser altimetry",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "ice surface elevation changes",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "2003\u20132010 elevation changes from air- and space-borne laser altimetry. To detect ice surface elevation changes from April 2003 to April 2010, which serves as the base data set from which to calculate a scale value, we use all available Ice, Cloud, and land Elevation Satellite (ICESat) GLA12 Release 31 data36. ICESat elevations have a crossover standard deviation of ICESat = 0.2 m (refs 37-39).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Airborne Topographic Mapper",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "surface elevation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The merged ATM and ICESat data cover outlet glaciers of variable size and termination regime. At these data points, elevations from the 1978\u201387 DEMs are extracted, although we remove interpolated DEM surfaces using a reliability mask18, an output produced during DEM production. The point-based difference between the ATM/ICESat measured surface elevation and the DEM elevation is dh80s-10.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SMB reconstruction of ref. 10",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "physically based meltwater retention scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Refinements are applied to the original SMB reconstruction10 as follows. (1) Values are now estimated over all land, sea, and ice within the domain, rather than over only ice. (2) A physically based meltwater retention scheme54 replaces the original simpler approach. (3) Multiple stations now contribute to the T value for each given month and grid cell within the domain, rather than employing the single highest-correlating station. (4) The RACMO2.1/GR data used for calibration have a higher native resolution (~11km) than the Polar MM5 data (~24km) used to calibrate the original SMB reconstruction. (5) The SMB reconstruction now extends to 2012, rather than 2010. (6) The ice-core-derived annual accumulation rates are divided into monthly temporal resolution by weighting the monthly fraction of annual accumulation after the 1960-2012 average RACMO2.1/GR seasonal distribution at each grid cell.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "RACMO2.1/GR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "surface mass balance",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Using SMB fields from RACMO2.1/GR output11 the elevation change due to firn compaction is calculated18,42 and subtracted from the total elevation change (dh2003\u20132010), thereby yielding an elevation change due to solid ice changes (dhsolid) on a 1 km \u00d7 1 km grid.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "firn compaction model",
            "source_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "elevation change due to solid-ice changes",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "SMB modelling. The near-surface air temperature T and the land-ice SMB (that is, total precipitation minus total sublimation minus runoff) reconstruction of ref. 10, spanning 1840-2012, is calibrated to RACMO2.1/GR output\u00b9\u00b9. The calibration is important because SMB fields from RACMO2.1/GR are used as input to convert total elevation during 2003-2010 (dh2003-2010) into elevation change due to solid-ice changes (dhsolid) using a firn-compaction model (see Methods section '2003-2010 elevation changes from air- and space-borne laser altimetry'). Thus, because we use dhsolid to calculate mass balance estimates during LIAmax-1983 and 1983-2003, it is critical that the two SMB models are comparable when assessing the components of the twentieth-century mass balance.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
